question_text:
Write a C++ function to compute $x^n$ using a recursive function. Your program should consist of the main function from which the user is requested for inputs, $x$ and $n$, and an additional function, f_recursive(), that implements $x^n$. A part of the algorithm for $x^n$ is as shown below:
If n is equal to 0 then return 1;
If n is equal to 1 then return x;
If n is even then ...
If n is odd then ...

Given:
string my_string;
Briefly explain the main difference between the following two statements:
cin << my_string;
getline(cin, my_string).

Please find out what is wrong with the if statement listed below, and rewrite it to fix that logical error. (Hint: what if someone's GPA is 1.0). Your new if statement should contain nested if-else-if statements.
if ((gpa >= 2.3) && (gpa < 3.0))
cout << "Your GPA is not bad but you should improve it.";
if ((gpa >= 3) && (gpa <= 3.7))
cout << "You got a pretty good GPA";
else
cout << “Congratulations. You made the Dean's list”;

The main purpose of C++ programming is to add object orientation to the C programming language and classes are the central feature of C++ that supports object-oriented programming. Using the code segment below explain the definition and working of C++ classes.
class Box {
public:
double length; // Length of box
double breadth; // Breadth of a box
double height; // Height of a box
};
